WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.699
Template, code, and expression injection risks appear when untrusted input can influence executable logic rather than plain content.

2
00:00:08.699 --> 00:00:20.046
Server-side template engines, expression evaluators, dynamic imports, plugin systems, and JavaScript evaluation features can all create sensitive interpretation points.

3
00:00:20.046 --> 00:00:28.670
The core question is whether the user controls data, or whether the user controls something the runtime treats as code or instructions.

4
00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:37.596
JavaScript offers powerful dynamic features, including eval-style behavior and constructors that can treat strings as executable code.

5
00:00:37.596 --> 00:00:41.681
These features are rarely appropriate for ordinary user input.

6
00:00:41.681 --> 00:00:50.682
Even when they seem convenient for rules, formulas, or customization, they can create a path where untrusted text changes program behavior.

7
00:00:50.682 --> 00:00:58.549
Safer designs use explicit configuration, constrained rule formats, or purpose-built parsers with limited capability.

8
00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:00.895
Template engines also require care.

9
00:01:00.895 --> 00:01:07.022
Rendering user content with a template is different from allowing users to define the template.

10
00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:16.477
If users can supply template syntax, expressions, or partials, the application must treat that as a high-risk design and apply strong constraints.

11
00:01:16.477 --> 00:01:25.404
Sandbox claims should be reviewed skeptically because sandboxes often have assumptions, escape conditions, or dependency limitations.

12
00:01:25.404 --> 00:01:31.228
Dependencies matter because libraries may interpret strings in ways that are not obvious.

13
00:01:31.228 --> 00:01:37.885
A validation library, markdown renderer, expression engine, or template helper may have its own rules.

14
00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:47.265
Reviewing dependency documentation and configuration is part of injection prevention when the dependency processes user-controlled strings.

15
00:01:47.265 --> 00:01:49.459
The safest pattern is separation.

16
00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:51.804
User content should remain content.

17
00:01:51.804 --> 00:01:55.284
Application logic should remain application-owned.

18
00:01:55.284 --> 00:02:02.621
If customization is required, expose a narrow, documented set of options rather than a general-purpose language.

19
00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:07.992
That design choice prevents untrusted users from turning configuration into code.
